Good, there's no existing mirror_mad_phantasm.md file. I need to create one. Let me also double check the `draw_top_card` issue one more time. The key question is: is the loop calling `draw_top_card()` when the library is ALREADY empty (after all cards are revealed)?

```rust
loop {
    let top = state.get_player_mut(owner).draw_top_card();
    match top {
        Some(card_id) => {
            let name = state.get_object(card_id).map(|o| o.name.clone()).unwrap_or_default();
            if name == "Mirror-Mad Phantasm" {
                found = Some(card_id);
                break;
            } else {
                milled.push(card_id);
            }
        }
        None => break, // Library empty.
    }
}
```

The `None` branch only triggers when the library is exhausted, at which point `draw_top_card()` returns `None` and sets `has_drawn_from_empty = true`. So if Mirror-Mad Phantasm isn't found before the library empties, the loop breaks after that final `draw_top_card()` call.

Now I'm thinking through the actual game scenarios. In the normal case where Mirror-Mad Phantasm activates its own ability, it gets shuffled into the library first, so the reveal loop finds it before exhausting the deck and never sets that flag. The bug only manifests in edge cases like Necrotic Ooze activating the ability when Mirror-Mad Phantasm isn't in the library at all.

I'm also considering what happens with multiple copies of Mirror-Mad Phantasm—if one activates the ability, the other copies would still be found during the reveal loop before the library empties, so that scenario wouldn't trigger the bug either. one that activated shuffles in, and the reveal loop finds one of them (the first one it encounters). The other one stays in the library. No issue.

What if there are TWO Mirror-Mad Phantasms and BOTH activate the ability simultaneously... but MTG doesn't allow simultaneous activation of the same ability by the same player. This is not a concern.

OK so the bug is specifically for the Necrotic Ooze / token-without-MMP-in-library edge case.

Now let me also check: is the loop's termination condition correct? The loop continues until EITHER:
1. A card named "Mirror-Mad Phantasm" is found (correct)
2. The library is empty (correct - but with the `has_drawn_from_empty` side effect)

These are the only two termination conditions per the oracle text. The issue is that when the library empties, the final `draw_top_card()` call sets `has_drawn_from_empty = true` as a side effect, which is the actual bug. Let me trace through what happens: after all cards are revealed without finding Mirror-Mad Phantasm, the library is empty, and the next `draw_top_card()` returns `None` while also setting that flag. This is the last call in the loop, and it's what's causing the problem. I'm writing up the bug report now, documenting how the reveal loop incorrectly triggers the empty library loss condition by calling `draw_top_card()` one final time after all cards are exhausted.
